cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Maestro: Human-Centered AI for Composition — A Literature Review and Taxonomy"
message: "If you use this survey or taxonomy, please cite it as below."
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: Tan
    given-names: Henry
    affiliation: Harvard University
abstract: >-
  A survey of 463 works (1957–2026) on AI for music composition, organized not by model
  architecture but by where each contribution sits in a composer's working loop —
  compose, annotate, compile, edit, play. Comprises a 43,900-word literature review, a
  taxonomy of 49 nodes and 10 coding dimensions, an annotated bibliography of 394 records
  with per-entry verification levels, and twelve open research directions. Argues that
  infilling under hard constraints, not text-to-song generation, is the primitive that
  supports composer-centered work, and identifies the annotation layer as the field's
  principal gap.
keywords:
  - music information retrieval
  - symbolic music generation
  - human-AI co-creation
  - creativity support tools
  - computational musicology
  - music notation
  - literature survey
license: CC-BY-4.0
